    <item>
      <title>Closed Suggestions</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/participate/community/acsp/suggestions/closed/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 14 May 2018 11:15:39 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/participate/community/acsp/suggestions/closed/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The following suggestions were closed because they were withdrawn by the submitter, out of scope, spam, inadvertent confirmations, or internal testing, and so no response was required:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;row-list&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;col-12 col-sm-4&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2006.7&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2007.20&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2007.33&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2008.4&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2008.19&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2009.1&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2009.8&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2009.14&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2009.18&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2010.17&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2011.33&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2013.3&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2013.17&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2013.18&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2013.19&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2013.23&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2013.25&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2013.31&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2013.32&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;col-12 col-sm-4&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2014.5&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2014.15&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2014.16&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2014.17&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2014.23&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2015.5&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2015.14&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2015.19&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2017.4&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2017.10&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2017.26&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2018.23&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2018.24&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2018.25&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2019.4&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2019.5&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2019.6&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2019.18&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;col-12 col-sm-4&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2020.7&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2020.8&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2021.9&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2021.11&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2021.12&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2022.3&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2022.9&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2022.17&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2022.19&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2023.03&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2023.04&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2023.05&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2023.06&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2023.07&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2023.14&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2023.19&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2025.1&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2025.2&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;2026.2&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Security Practices</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/reference/materials/trust/security/practices/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 10 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/reference/materials/trust/security/practices/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://www.arin.net/reference/materials/trust/security/practices.png&#34; alt=&#34;Security Practices&#34; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Information security and data protection, which are critical to defending against threats such as fraud, hacking, and phishing, have always been a priority at ARIN. We have dedicated significant resources to ensuring secure system design and the careful safeguarding of customer data.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Click on each link to expand/for more information&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;access-and-data-protection&#34;&gt;Access and Data Protection&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;card collapse-card closed&#34; aria-label=&#34;Click for more information about Access Control &amp;amp;amp; MFA&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;div class=&#34;card-header collapse-card-blue&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;a href=&#34;javascript:void(0)&#34; aria-expanded=&#34;false&#34; aria-controls=&#34;cc-35ad2000699198431919cd0160a467d4&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;i class=&#34;fa fa-chevron-right&#34; aria-hidden=&#34;true&#34;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;Access Control &amp;amp; MFA&#xA;  &lt;/a&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/div&gt;&#xA;  &lt;div class=&#34;card-body&#34; id=&#34;cc-35ad2000699198431919cd0160a467d4&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We verify user identity and enforce rules regarding which resources they are permitted to view, use, or modify in order to safeguard sensitive data and physical spaces. ARIN requires the use of multi-factor authentication (MFA). &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/reference/materials/security/multifactor/&#34;&gt;&amp;raquo;Learn more&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>IPv6 Web Usage in the ARIN Region</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/reference/research/statistics/ipv6_usage/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 01 Apr 2026 13:39:26 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/reference/research/statistics/ipv6_usage/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;The following report offers a quarterly snapshot of IPv6 web usage traffic across the &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/about/welcome/region/&#34;&gt;ARIN region&lt;/a&gt; by geographic location across multiple sources.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;ipv6-web-usage-in-the-arin-region&#34;&gt;IPv6 Web Usage in the ARIN Region&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&#xA;  &#xA;  &#xA;  &#xA;  &#xA;  &#xA;  &lt;div class=&#34;card panel-secondary panel-table&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;table class=&#39;table table-striped table-bordered table-sortable js-data-responsive&#39;&gt;&lt;caption&gt;IPv6 Web Usage by Geographic Locations in the ARIN Region&lt;/caption&gt;&#xA;  &lt;thead&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;th scope=&#39;col&#39;&gt;Geographic Location in ARIN Region&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;          &lt;th scope=&#39;col&#39;&gt;IPv6 Web Usage&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/thead&gt;&#xA;  &lt;tbody&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Anguilla&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;32.74%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Antarctica&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;11.02%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Antigua and Barbuda&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;01.86%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Bahamas&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;31.80%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Barbados&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;17.51%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Bermuda&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;17.65%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Bouvet Island&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;00.00%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Canada&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;43.77%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Cayman Islands&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;01.67%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Dominica&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;38.95%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Grenada&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;07.10%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Guadeloupe&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;48.00%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Heard and McDonald Islands&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;100.00%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Jamaica&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;17.36%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Martinique&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;38.90%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Montserrat&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;12.86%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Puerto Rico&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;55.48%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Saint Barthelemy&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;68.62%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Saint Helena&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;66.97%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Saint Kitts and Nevis&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;00.96%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Saint Lucia&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;16.55%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Saint Martin&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;20.03%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Saint Pierre and Miquelon&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;00.58%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Saint Vincent and The Grenadines&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;10.95%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Turks and Caicos Islands&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;31.08%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;United States&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;59.40%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;United States Minor Outlying Islands&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;00.00%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Virgin Islands (British)&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;17.05%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Virgin Islands (U.S.)&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;11.85%&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/tbody&gt;&#xA;&lt;/table&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;card well&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;div class=&#34;card-body&#34;&gt;&#xA;  IPv6 web usage statistics were calculated using data from &lt;a href=&#34;https://stats.labs.apnic.net/ipv6&#34;&gt;APNIC&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.google.com/intl/en/ipv6/statistics.html&#34;&gt;Google&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.facebook.com/ipv6/?tab=ipv6_country&#34;&gt;Meta&lt;/a&gt;, with an average taken of non-null values per reported country code. This table is updated quarterly.&#xA;  &lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>ARIN Online IRR User Guide</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/irr/userguide/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 29 Mar 2026 15:12:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/irr/userguide/</guid>
      <description>&lt;!--&lt;div class=&#34;infobox&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note&lt;/strong&gt;: As of 4 April 2022, users will no longer be able to create, update, or delete records in the ARIN-NONAUTH database, the email template entry method will no longer be available, and the ARIN-NONAUTH data stream will no longer be available in Near Real Time Mirroring (NRTM) or Whois Port 43. &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/announcements/20220404-irr/&#34;&gt;Please see our announcement&lt;/a&gt; for more information.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;--&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;overview&#34;&gt;Overview&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;ARIN&amp;rsquo;s IRR is a searchable database of routing policy information for networks in the ARIN region. Objects in the IRR contain routing information in the specific format of Routing Policy Specification Language (RPSL). ARIN Online provides a method to create IRR objects by entering information into a window in the graphical user interface (GUI), and the corresponding RPSL objects are created by the system. This method of managing IRR objects is referred to as IRR-online.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Managing Incoming Assignments</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/incoming/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 09 Dec 2025 09:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/incoming/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;introduction&#34;&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;With the January 2026 release, ARIN gives organizations control over whether to receive reassignments and reallocations from other organizations. This feature is designed to protect the recipient organization against bad actors and from reputational damage. When an Org ID is set to not accept reassignments, any attempt by a resource holder to reassign or reallocate to that Org ID will be rejected.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;By default, all Org IDs are set to accept reassignments. No action is required to continue accepting reassignments.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Using ARIN’s RPKI with Bring Your Own IP Services</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/rpki/help/byoip/</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 31 Aug 2024 09:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/rpki/help/byoip/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Bring Your Own IP (BYOIP) is a process available from multiple service providers where you can bring your own IP prefix to an upstream ISP, carrier, or cloud provider, and that upstream provider can advertise your prefix from their network.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;ARIN plays an integral role in the configuration and deployment of BYOIP for some service providers, particularly in the creation of &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/rpki/roa_request/&#34;&gt;Route Origin Authorizations (ROAs)&lt;/a&gt;. These records allow a resource holder to authorize an upstream provider to advertise its downstream customer’s prefix from its Autonomous System Number (ASN).&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Current Fee Schedule FAQ</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/fees/fee_schedule/faq/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 12 Sep 2023 00:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/fees/fee_schedule/faq/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;col&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;div class=&#34;card panel-light&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;div class=&#34;card-header&#34;&gt;&#xA;      &lt;p class=&#34;panel-title h2&#34;&gt;Changes to the Fee Schedule&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/div&gt;&#xA;    &lt;div class=&#34;card-body&#34;&gt;&#xA;      &lt;h2 id=&#34;what-changes-took-effect-on-1-january-2026-when-arin-moved-to-the-new-fee-schedule&#34;&gt;What changes took effect on 1 January 2026 when ARIN moved to the new Fee Schedule?&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;The Board of Trustees increased Registration Services Plan fees for each service category by 5%.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;There were no changes to transactional fees.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;!--&#xA;## Why did the Fee Schedule change in 2024? &#xA;&#xA;In 2022, ARIN transitioned end user customers to the Registration Services Plan (RSP) fee schedule, based on total IPv4 and IPv6 resources held, to ensure costs were distributed in an equitable manner by eliminating the fee differentiation between ISP and end user organizations. Now, ARIN completes that fee harmonization process by transitioning Autonomous System Numbers (ASNs) to the RSP Fee Schedule. &#xA;&#xA;The new Fee Schedule includes the following changes:  &#xA;&#xA;- Transitioning ASNs to the RSP Fee Schedule &#xA;- Eliminating the $550 ASN issuance fee for new ASNs  &#xA; &#xA;This change also transitions our ASN-only customers to become ARIN Service Members, making them eligible to become General Members and participate in ARIN governance if they so choose.  &#xA;&#xA;The new ARIN Fee Schedule brings all ARIN customers into a unified, equitable fee schedule, and provides equal standing to all ARIN customers with Internet number resources that are held under an ARIN Agreement (RSA/LRSA). &#xA;--&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;what-resources-are-available-if-we-have-additional-questions&#34;&gt;What resources are available if we have additional questions?&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Please see the following links for more information. If you have additional questions, please contact &lt;a href=&#34;mailto:fee-inquiries@arin.net&#34;&gt;fee-inquiries@arin.net&lt;/a&gt; or submit via Ask ARIN while logged into your ARIN Online account.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Qualified Facilitator Program Terms of Use</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/transfers/facilitators/tou/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 24 May 2023 00:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/transfers/facilitators/tou/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Version 1.0 (June 2023)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;These Terms of Use (“ToU”) for the Qualified Facilitator Program (“QFP”) describe the terms and conditions that govern your use of the American Registry for Internet Numbers, Ltd.’s (“ARIN”) Qualified Facilitator Program.  By accessing or using the QFP in any manner, including without limitation, as a holder of IPv4 or Autonomous System Number (ASN) resources, a potential recipient of IPv4 or Autonomous System Number (ASN) resources, or a Qualified Facilitator (collectively, “QFP Participant”), you accept and agree to these ToU.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>2022 ARIN Community Grant Program Recipients</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/about/community_grants/recipients/2022/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 21 Sep 2022 09:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/about/community_grants/recipients/2022/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;2022-arin-community-grant-program-recipients&#34;&gt;2022 ARIN Community Grant Program Recipients&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;table class=&#39;table table-striped table-bordered js-data-responsive&#39;&gt;&#xA;&lt;caption&gt;2022 Applications&lt;/caption&gt;&#xA;&lt;tbody&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;th scope=&#34;row&#34;&gt;Applications&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;td&gt;14&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;th scope=&#34;row&#34;&gt;Organization Type&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;td&gt;3 associations, 3 corporations, 6 nonprofits, 1 foundation, 1 university &lt;/td&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;th scope=&#34;row&#34;&gt;Organization Region&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;td&gt;5 United States, 4 Caribbean, 5 outside ARIN region&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;th scope=&#34;row&#34;&gt;Category (some projects identified multiple categories)&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;td&gt;1 Internet technical improvements, 1 registry processes and technology improvements, 5 informational outreach, 8 research&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;th scope=&#34;row&#34;&gt;Total funding requested (USD)&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;td&gt;$235,813.60&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;th scope=&#34;row&#34;&gt;Average funding requested (USD)&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;td&gt;$16,843&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;tr class=&#34;table-info&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;th scope=&#34;row&#34;&gt;Projects selected to receive a grant&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;td&gt;3&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;tr class=&#34;table-info&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;th scope=&#34;row&#34;&gt;Total funding provided (USD)&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;td&gt;$44,500&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/tbody&gt;&#xA;&lt;/table&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Project reports provided by grant recipients.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Open-source Template Processor (TP)</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/ostp/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 31 Aug 2022 09:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/ostp/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;infobox&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;ARIN retired its original email template processor &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/announcements/20240111-templates&#34;&gt;on 3 June 2024&lt;/a&gt;. If you are an ARIN customer who relied on templates to manage your registration records, we encourage you to use our Reg-RWS service.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For organizations who used &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/#swip&#34;&gt;SWIP email templates&lt;/a&gt; to manage their &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/#reporting-reassignments&#34;&gt;registration records&lt;/a&gt;, ARIN also offers an open-source Template Processor (TP) as an alternative method to submit &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/#swip&#34;&gt;SWIP email templates&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This open-source software can help you provide network reassignment/reallocation information to ARIN using traditional email templates. You will need to send the email templates to an email server that you run, and this software will convert and submit them to ARIN using ARIN’s RESTful API.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Open-source Template Processor (TP)</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/ostp/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 31 Aug 2022 09:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/ostp/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;infobox&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;ARIN retired its original email template processor &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/announcements/20240111-templates&#34;&gt;on 3 June 2024&lt;/a&gt;. If you are an ARIN customer who relied on templates to manage your registration records, we encourage you to use our Reg-RWS service.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For organizations who used &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/#swip&#34;&gt;SWIP email templates&lt;/a&gt; to manage their &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/#reporting-reassignments&#34;&gt;registration records&lt;/a&gt;, ARIN also offers an open-source Template Processor (TP) as an alternative method to submit &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/#swip&#34;&gt;SWIP email templates&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This open-source software can help you provide network reassignment/reallocation information to ARIN using traditional email templates. You will need to send the email templates to an email server that you run, and this software will convert and submit them to ARIN using ARIN’s RESTful API.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Repository Publication Service (RPS)</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/rpki/options/rps/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 24 Jun 2022 00:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/rpki/options/rps/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;what-is-arin-s-repository-publication-service-rps&#34;&gt;What is ARIN&amp;rsquo;s Repository Publication Service (RPS)?&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;As stated in &lt;a href=&#34;https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8181&#34;&gt;RFC8181&lt;/a&gt;, in order to make participation in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) easier, it is helpful to have a few consolidated repositories for RPKI objects, thus saving every participant from the cost of maintaining a new service. Similarly, Relying Parties using the RPKI objects will find it faster and more reliable to retrieve the necessary set from a smaller number of repositories.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>ARIN Fellowship Program Terms and Conditions</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/participate/meetings/fellowships/terms/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 06 Jan 2021 00:00:00 -0500</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/participate/meetings/fellowships/terms/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;1-program-overview&#34;&gt;1. Program Overview&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The ARIN Fellowship Program (the “Program”) includes virtual educational sessions and mentorship opportunities in the lead-up to each biannual ARIN Public Policy and Members Meeting (the “Meeting”). Fellows are selected by community volunteers on the ARIN Fellowship Selection Committee in one of two categories: In-Person Fellows or Virtual Fellows.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In-Person Fellows receive financial sponsorship, including a travel stipend and certain  travel expenses to attend a Meeting on-site. Virtual Fellows participate fully in all educational sessions and program content but do not receive financial sponsorship, as they will participate virtually.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Detailed Reassignment and Reallocations</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/templates/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 06 Feb 2020 00:00:00 -0500</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/reassignments/templates/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;ARIN customers can report reallocation/reassignment data by using the &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/regrws/&#34;&gt;Reg-RWS Application Programming Interface (API)&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;reg-rws-api-reassignment-reallocation-status-messages&#34;&gt;Reg-RWS API Reassignment/Reallocation Status Messages&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3 id=&#34;downstream-org-id-does-not-exist-in-whois&#34;&gt;Downstream Org ID Does Not Exist in Whois&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This message is sent to the email address of the user whose API Key is included in the request. (The API Key is specified in the REST call that was sent to ARIN&amp;rsquo;s Reg-RWS system.)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;card well&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;div class=&#34;card-body&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Hello,&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is courtesy message to notify you that the reassignment of &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;IP-ADDRESSES&lt;/code&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/em&gt; to an organization with Org ID &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;ORG-HANDLE&lt;/code&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/em&gt; was not successful. The reassignment could not be processed because the downstream Organization Identifier (Org ID) provided does not exist in ARIN&amp;rsquo;s Whois database. Please review ARIN&amp;rsquo;s Whois to verify the correct downstream Org ID and resubmit your reassignment request.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Endorsements/Acknowledgments</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/about/relations/governance/endorsements/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 25 Apr 2019 09:31:51 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/about/relations/governance/endorsements/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;card panel-secondary panel-table&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;table class=&#39;table table-striped table-bordered table-sortable js-data-responsive&#39;&gt;&lt;caption&gt;Endorsements and Acknowledgments&lt;/caption&gt;&#xA;  &lt;thead&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;th scope=&#39;col&#39;&gt;Name&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;          &lt;th scope=&#39;col&#39;&gt;Description&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;          &lt;th scope=&#39;col&#39;&gt;Date&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/thead&gt;&#xA;  &lt;tbody&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/about/relations/governance/pdf/stkittsnevis_ARIN.pdf&#34;&gt;Letter from the Government of St Kitts and Nevis&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Ministry of Information letter of Affirmation of Collaboration&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;26 September 2025&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/about/relations/governance/pdf/Mendicino_ARIN.pdf&#34;&gt;Letter from the Honourable Marco Mendicino&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Public Safety Canada letter of appreciation&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;23 March 2023&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/about/relations/governance/pdf/Blair_ARIN.pdf&#34;&gt;Letter from the Honourable Bill Blair&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Canada Ministry of Public Safety and Emergency Preparedness letter of appreciation&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;18 December 2020&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/about/relations/governance/pdf/HRes717_50th_Ann_Internet.pdf&#34;&gt;Letter from Rep. Eshoo and H.Res.717&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Recognizing the 50th anniversary of the Internet&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;21 November 2019&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://www.ntia.doc.gov/blog/2012/united-states-government-s-internet-protocol-numbering-principles&#34;&gt;United States Government’s Internet Protocol Numbering Principles&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;The American Registry for Internet Numbers (ARIN) is the RIR for Canada, many Caribbean and North Atlantic islands, and the United States. The USG participates in the development of and is supportive of the policies, processes, and procedures agreed upon by the Internet technical community through ARIN.&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;03 December 2012&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/tbody&gt;&#xA;&lt;/table&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Frequently Asked Questions for Law Enforcement and Public Safety Organizations</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/about/relations/law_enforcement/faq/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 12 Sep 2018 09:32:49 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/about/relations/law_enforcement/faq/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;col&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;div class=&#34;card panel-style panel-default&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;div class=&#34;card-header&#34;&gt;&#xA;      &lt;h2 class=&#34;card-title&#34;&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/div&gt;&#xA;    &lt;div class=&#34;card-body&#34;&gt;&#xA;      &lt;p&gt;The American Registry for Internet Numbers, Ltd. (ARIN) is one of five Regional Internet Registries (RIR) that serves the global Internet community by distributing and managing Internet number resources (IPv4, IPv6, and Autonomous System Numbers). The RIRs are referred to collectively as the Number Resource Organization (NRO), whose mandate is to actively contribute to an open, stable, and secure Internet.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Learn more about the &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.nro.net/&#34;&gt;Number Resource Organization&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;View more information on the &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/about/welcome/region/#regional-internet-registry-regions&#34;&gt;RIR registries and their respective coverage areas&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;ARIN manages the distribution of Internet number resources within its defined service region, which includes Canada, parts of the Caribbean and North Atlantic, and the United States.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>IP Address Blocks ARIN Issues From</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/reference/research/statistics/ip_blocks/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 10 Sep 2018 13:38:52 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/reference/research/statistics/ip_blocks/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;arin-ipv4-address-blocks&#34;&gt;ARIN IPv4 Address Blocks&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;ARIN (or its predecessor registries) have performed allocation and assignment of IPv4 address blocks from specific address blocks assigned by IANA. The list of IPv4 address blocks is available &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.iana.org/numbers&#34;&gt;here on the IANA website.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;ARIN&amp;rsquo;s free pool of IPv4 address space was depleted on &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/vault/announcements/20150924/&#34;&gt;24 September 2015&lt;/a&gt;. As a result, we are no longer fulfilling requests for IPv4 addresses directly from a free pool. All approved IPv4 requests are placed on our &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/guide/ipv4/waiting_list/&#34;&gt;waiting list for unmet requests&lt;/a&gt;. There are exceptions in special cases outlined by policy, such as:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Route Origin Authorizations (ROAs)</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/rpki/roas/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 05 Sep 2018 12:58:38 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/rpki/roas/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;roa-overview&#34;&gt;Route Origin Authorization (ROA) Overview&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A ROA is a cryptographically signed object that states which Autonomous System Number (ASN) is authorized to originate a particular IP address prefix or set of prefixes. ROAs may only be generated for Internet number resources covered by your resource certificate. Reallocated and reassigned net resources cannot be added to an organization&amp;rsquo;s RPKI certificate. (The term &lt;em&gt;ROA Request&lt;/em&gt; is used interchangeably with &lt;em&gt;ROA&lt;/em&gt; on ARIN&amp;rsquo;s site to mean a route origination authorization created in ARIN&amp;rsquo;s RPKI repository.)&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>RESTful Payloads</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/regrws/payloads/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 04 Sep 2018 09:35:58 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/regrws/payloads/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;introduction&#34;&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When using &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/regrws/methods/&#34;&gt;RESTful methods&lt;/a&gt; that alter database records without deleting them (for example, &lt;code&gt;PUT&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;POST&lt;/code&gt;), Reg-RWS will require more information in the form of &lt;strong&gt;properly formatted Extensible Markup Language (XML) layouts&lt;/strong&gt;, called &lt;strong&gt;payloads&lt;/strong&gt;. These payloads contain details about the record being created or modified, including organization information, POC information, IP address ranges, customer data, etc. Each entry below explains a particular XML payload and provides an example of said payload completed using sample information. Each entry also shows which fields may not be modified or are generated by Reg-RWS automatically. (XML payloads for Internet Routing Registry (IRR) commands are described in the &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/irr/irr-restful/&#34;&gt;ARIN IRR RESTful API User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.)&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Reporting a Whois Inaccuracy</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/whois/inaccuracy_reporting/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 30 Aug 2018 10:42:04 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/whois/inaccuracy_reporting/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;If you’ve found an inaccuracy in &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/whois/&#34;&gt;ARIN’s public Whois&lt;/a&gt;, you have a few options for correcting the error, depending on if the record is your own or someone else’s.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;correcting-your-own-whois-record&#34;&gt;Correcting Your Own Whois Record&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If you’ve discovered a mistake on your own Whois record, you can correct the inaccuracy yourself using ARIN Online. Simply log in, choose &lt;strong&gt;Your Records&lt;/strong&gt; from the navigation menu, and then choose the record type that needs to be updated. If you do not have an &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/guide/account/&#34;&gt;ARIN Online account&lt;/a&gt;, you may create one by choosing &lt;strong&gt;Log In&lt;/strong&gt; on any ARIN page and choosing the &lt;strong&gt;Create User Account&lt;/strong&gt; option.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Whois-RWS FAQs</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/whois/rws/faq/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 30 Aug 2018 10:41:41 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/whois/rws/faq/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;card well&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;div class=&#34;card-body&#34;&gt;&#xA;  As of 2019, ARIN strongly recommends using &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/registry/whois/rdap/&#34;&gt;Whois/RDAP&lt;/a&gt; because of its standardization, support for security, and access to registration information from other RIRs, registrars, and organizations who support RDAP.&#xA;  &lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;what-is-whois-rws&#34;&gt;What is Whois-RWS?&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;ARIN provides a Representational State Transfer (REST)ful interface for Whois queries (Whois-RWS). RESTful systems usually utilize URLs that are sent using an Application Programming Interface (API). ARIN&amp;rsquo;s Whois-RWS can be accessed through its web interface with a browser or through command line scripts.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
