    <item>
      <title>Publication of Received Nominations</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/announcements/20260625/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 25 Jun 2026 00:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/announcements/20260625/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;The call for nominations for the 2026 ARIN Elections closed as scheduled at 7:00 PM ET on 22 June. Having met all requirements established in the Election Process, the self-nomination period is now complete.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The following nominees submitted the required information to be considered as candidates for the Board of Trustees and Advisory Council (AC), and for the appointment of one seat to the Number Resource Organization Number Council (NRO NC).&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Navin Suvarna</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/blog/author/navin-suvarna/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 24 Jun 2026 15:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/blog/author/navin-suvarna/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Navin Suvarna is based in Research Triangle Park (RTP), North Carolina, and focuses on large-scale network architecture, automation, routing security, AI-assisted operations, and operational resiliency across enterprise and public sector environments. He is a Cisco Press author, patent holder, and active industry speaker, with speaking engagements at NANOG, Cisco Live, and other technical forums.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Bridging Policy and Operations: An ARIN 57 Fellowship Experience</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/blog/2026/06/24/fellowship-experience-navin-suvarna/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 24 Jun 2026 14:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://www.arin.net/blog/2026/06/24/fellowship-experience-navin-suvarna/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Attending &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/ARIN57/&#34;&gt;ARIN 57&lt;/a&gt; as a Fellow gave me the opportunity to engage directly with the leaders, operators, engineers, researchers, and policy contributors who help shape the future of Internet infrastructure, routing security, and number resource governance. The week was both professionally rewarding and personally meaningful, and it left me with a clearer view of how policy and operations come together to keep the Internet resilient.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A consistent thread throughout the meeting was that Internet number resource policy, routing security, and operational execution are increasingly interconnected. Discussions about &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/guide/ipv4/&#34;&gt;IPv4&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/guide/ipv6/&#34;&gt;IPv6&lt;/a&gt;, Resource Public Key Infrastructure (&lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/rpki/&#34;&gt;RPKI&lt;/a&gt;), Autonomous System Provider Authorizations (&lt;a href=&#34;https://www.arin.net/resources/manage/rpki/aspa/&#34;&gt;ASPAs&lt;/a&gt;), and emerging areas such as space networking reinforced that long-term Internet resilience depends on aligning policy evolution with operational reality.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>22nd Caribbean Internet Governance Forum</title>
      <link>https://www.arin.net/events/CaribbeanIGF22/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 24 Jun 2026 00:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
